meta {
  name: Quantities
  seq: 9
}

auth {
  mode: inherit
}

docs {
  The second service, `recordsimilarity.v1.Quantities`: the unit conversion and the comparison that `RecordSimilarity`
  applies to a record's quantities, for a caller that has amounts and no record, and the reading of an amount that a
  caller holds as text.
  
  Nothing here reads or writes a record, runs the model, or looks at the server's schema or limits. Every call is
  answered from its request alone, the same request always gets the same answer, and the server answers these while it
  is not ready for anything else. The ten requests can be sent in any order, on a server in any state.
  
  | Request | Call | What it shows |
  |---|---|---|
  | `Compare` | `Compare` | A change of amount, and the same amount in another unit. |
  | `Compare [Restored]` | `Compare` | A quantity that comes back, compared with its first value. |
  | `Compare [Incomparable]` | `Compare` | Units that do not compare, and a unit the server does not convert. |
  | `Convert` | `Convert` | A quantity written in another unit. |
  | `Convert [Base Unit]` | `Convert` | A quantity written in the unit its dimension is compared in. |
  | `Convert [Does Not Convert]` | `Convert` | Two units that measure different things. |
  | `ListUnits` | `ListUnits` | Every unit the server converts. |
  | `Parse` | `Parse` | Amounts in every form that is read. |
  | `Parse [Weight Words]` | `Parse` | The weights the trade names by a word. |
  | `Parse [Refused]` | `Parse` | One text for each kind of refusal. |
  
  What a call computes is exactly what `Evaluate` reports for the same amounts, except that `beyond_limit` is never
  set, since a limit belongs to a schema. A unit the server does not convert is never an error: it is comparable with
  itself and with nothing else.
  
  ### A quantity (`Quantity`)
  
  A measured amount with its unit: a weight, a volume, a potency. A single value is a range whose two ends are equal,
  and a caller that has only one end of a range sends it as both.
  
  | Field | Type | Values |
  |---|---|---|
  | `min` | double | The low end. A finite number, 0 or more. |
  | `max` | double | The high end. A finite number, not below `min`. |
  | `unit` | string | The unit both ends are in. Not blank. Matched without regard to case or the space around it: `G` and `g` are one unit. |
  
  These units are converted before amounts are compared, so `1 g` and `1000 mg` are the same quantity:
  
  | Measures | Compared in | Spellings |
  |---|---|---|
  | a mass | `mg` | `mcg` `µg` `μg` `microgram` `micrograms`, `mg` `milligram` `milligrams`, `g` `gram` `grams`, `kg` `kilogram` `kilograms`, `oz` `ounce` `ounces`, `lb` `lbs` `pound` `pounds` |
  | a volume | `ml` | `ml` `milliliter` `milliliters` `millilitre` `millilitres`, `cl` `centiliter` `centiliters`, `l` `liter` `liters` `litre` `litres`, `fl oz` `floz` `fl. oz` `fl. oz.` `fluid ounce` `fluid ounces` |
  | a share of the whole | `%` | `%` `percent` `pct` |
  
  Any other unit (`pack`, `each`, a misspelling) is accepted and never an error. It is comparable with itself and with
  nothing else. `Quantities` > `ListUnits` gives the table the server runs with, each unit with its factor.
}
